README.OSK -- what this disk changed in C NewsOne file: libcnews/config.c, whose four directory names were blarson'sown (/h0/ulib/news, /h0/ulib/newsbin, /h0/spool/news).  They are now/h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS, /h0/CMDS/NEWS and /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS; libcnews/config.c.oriis the original.  NEWSCTL, NEWSBIN, NEWSPATH and NEWSARTS in theenvironment still override them, as C News intends.relaynews, newsrun and libcnews.l build with the recipes in the project'stools/rebuild/recipes.psv.THREE FILES ARE ADDED, all in libosk and all written for this collection.libosk/linkcopy.c.  RBFhas no hard links, and blarslib's ln.c, which supplies link() otherwise,makes one by writing directory sectors on the raw device -- its headersays "USE AT YOUR OWN RISK", and under os9exec it left C News's locklooking like a directory.  C News links only for its lock and forcross-posted articles, so linkcopy.c creates the second name exclusively(failing if it exists, which the lock depends on) and copies the file.relaynews links it ahead of blarslib.l.libosk/renamecopy.c.  newsrun moves a refused batch into bad/ withrename(), and the only rename a link could find was blarslib's, in ln.cbeside that raw-sector link(): linking both clashed.  This rename() islinkcopy's link() and then an unlink() of the old name.libosk/trlf.c.  newsrun runs `trlf <file>' on a batch it has uncompressedand `trlf -s <in >-out' on one it has not -- "a blars special hack forOSK: inplace tr \l \n", in newsrun.c's own comment -- and the originalis in no archive.  This one turns LF into CR in place or from standardinput to standard output, and nothing more.